/**
 * @defgroup compressor_modeling_overview Compressor Modeling: From a Measured Point to Power and Airflow
 * @ingroup compressor_modeling
 * @brief An engineer-facing guide to the compressor performance models used by the assessment.
 * @details
 * These calculations turn one measured operating observation into compressor package power and
 * delivered airflow. They are performance-curve models, not equipment simulations: the selected
 * compressor construction and control method determine the curve shape, while catalog or measured
 * reference points establish its scale.
 *
 * Apply the following sequence independently to every interval in a measured profile:
 *
 * 1. Select the compressor construction and a compatible control method.
 * 2. Establish full-load, no-load, unload, turndown, and maximum-flow reference points as required.
 * 3. Correct the reference points for operating pressure and inlet conditions when applicable.
 * 4. Normalize the interval observation to power fraction or airflow fraction.
 * 5. Evaluate the control-specific normalized performance curve.
 * 6. Reconstruct package power in kW and delivered airflow in acfm.
 *
 * The result from one interval does not carry state into the next. Repeating these steps for every
 * measured interval produces a power and airflow profile suitable for energy and opportunity analysis.
 *
 * @heading{Applicability}
 *
 * | Compressor construction | Lubricant configuration | Supported control models | Important limits |
 * |---|---|---|---|
 * | Centrifugal | Not used by these models | Load/unload, blow-off, modulation/unload | Pressure correction uses the supplied three-point capacity curve. |
 * | Rotary screw | Lubricant-injected or oil-free | Load/unload, modulation with unload, variable displacement with unload, modulation without unload, start/stop, VFD | The shared pressure correction uses rated inlet pressure as its pressure-ratio base. |
 * | Reciprocating | `None` | Load/unload and start/stop | The shared pressure correction uses atmospheric pressure as its pressure-ratio base. |
 *
 * Storage volume is currently used by every load/unload cycle evaluation. Whether storage should be
 * restricted to narrower equipment configurations remains an engineering validation item; this
 * documentation describes the executable behavior rather than silently changing it.
 *
 * @heading{Where Inputs Come From}
 *
 * | Origin | Typical quantities | How to use them |
 * |---|---|---|
 * | User measurement | interval kW, voltage, current, power factor, acfm, operating pressure | Supplies the operating point evaluated for that interval. Power factor is a fraction from 0 to 1. |
 * | Manufacturer or catalog data | full-load acfm, BHP, package kW, pressure points, no-load power, turndown | Establishes reference anchors. A catalog value of -9999 means unavailable or not applicable and must not be used as a number. |
 * | User-selected or engineering default | compressor type, control, lubricant, storage, cycle timings | Selects the model and fills quantities that are not measured. Confirm defaults against the actual compressor controls. |
 * | Derived quantity | normalized fractions, corrected anchors, cycle times, stage energy | Calculated internally from the preceding inputs. |
 *
 * @heading{Common Outputs}
 * @copydoc compressor_assessment_result_formula
 *
 * @heading{Electrical Measurements}
 * @copydoc compressor_assessment_electrical_formula
 *
 * @heading{Pressure and Inlet Correction}
 * @copydoc compressor_assessment_pressure_inlet_formula
 *
 * @heading{Pressure and Power Language}
 * Brake horsepower (BHP) is mechanical shaft power; package kW is electrical input power and is
 * higher than shaft kW because the motor is not perfectly efficient. The rounded conversion used
 * by this library is 0.746 kW per bhp. A pressure marked psig is measured relative to the local
 * atmosphere. A pressure marked psia is absolute; pressure ratios must use absolute pressure.
 *
 * @heading{Assumptions and Limits}
 * - Reference values must describe the same compressor and operating configuration.
 * - Fractions are dimensionless values, normally from 0 to 1. Catalog percentages must be divided
 *   by 100 before use as fractions.
 * - The empirical pressure corrections and control curves are inherited engineering correlations;
 *   they do not replace a manufacturer performance map outside the documented range.
 * - Blow-off airflow remains available in the calculation interface because it is needed to balance
 *   the centrifugal model. In the engineering narrative it is a bypass-flow intermediate, not a
 *   primary MEASUR result.
 *
 * Source basis: <em>Compressed Air Compressor Modeling Calculations</em>, revision June 2020;
 * <em>Compressed Air Assessment Calculations</em>, revision June 2020; and
 * <em>Compressed Air Energy Efficiency Measure Calculations</em>, revision June 2020.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup compressor_assessment_result_formula Dimensional Result Reconstruction
 * @ingroup compressor_modeling_overview
 * @brief Convert normalized curve results back to package power and airflow.
 * @details
 * @formula{compressor-assessment-result-power;
 *   P = f_P P_{FL}
 * }
 * @formula{compressor-assessment-result-airflow;
 *   Q = f_Q Q_{FL}
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{P; Calculated package input power; \kilo\watt}
 * @symrow{Q; Calculated delivered airflow; \acfm}
 * @symrow{f_P; Full-load power fraction; \unitless}
 * @symrow{f_Q; Full-load airflow fraction; \unitless}
 * @symrow{P_{FL}; Corrected full-load package power; \kilo\watt}
 * @symrow{Q_{FL}; Corrected full-load airflow; \acfm}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * @subheading{Example}
 * If the selected curve returns @math{f_P=0.82} and @math{f_Q=0.60} for a compressor with
 * @math{P_{FL}=452.3\ \mathrm{kW}} and @math{Q_{FL}=3138\ \mathrm{acfm}}, then
 * @math{P=0.82(452.3)=370.9\ \mathrm{kW}} and
 * @math{Q=0.60(3138)=1882.8\ \mathrm{acfm}}.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup compressor_assessment_electrical_formula Three-Phase Electrical Measurement
 * @ingroup compressor_modeling_overview
 * @brief Convert line voltage, line current, and power factor to measured package power.
 * @details
 * @formula{compressor-assessment-electrical-power;
 *   P_{meas}=\frac{V I PF (1.732)}{1000}
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{P_{meas}; Measured three-phase package input power; \kilo\watt}
 * @symrow{V; Line-to-line voltage; \volt}
 * @symrow{I; Line current; \ampere}
 * @symrow{PF; Power factor expressed from 0 to 1; \unitless}
 * @symrow{1.732; Rounded square root of three retained by the source algorithm; \unitless}
 * @symrow{1000; Watts per kilowatt; \watt\per\kilo\watt}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * @subheading{Example}
 * At 440 V, 246.7 A, and @math{PF=0.50},
 * @math{P_{meas}=440(246.7)(0.50)(1.732)/1000=94.0\ \mathrm{kW}}. A value of 50 for power
 * factor is invalid here; 50 percent must be entered as 0.50.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup compressor_assessment_pressure_inlet_formula Pressure and Inlet Correction
 * @ingroup compressor_modeling_overview
 * @brief Correct positive-displacement reference power and airflow to operating conditions.
 * @details Centrifugal compressors instead use the three-point quadratic described on
 * @ref centrifugal_compressor_assessment. For screw compressors @math{P_b=P_{in,rated}}; for
 * reciprocating compressors @math{P_b=P_{atm}}.
 *
 * @formula{compressor-assessment-pressure-power;
 *   P_{adj}=F_P\frac{P_{atm}}{P_{in}}BHP_{FL}\frac{0.746}{\eta}R_P
 * }
 * @formula{compressor-assessment-pressure-flow;
 *   Q_{adj}=F_Q Q_{rated}\left[1-0.00075(P_d-P_{rated})\right]
 * }
 * @formula{compressor-assessment-pressure-ratio;
 *   R_P=\frac{[(P_d+P_b)/P_b]^{(n-1)/n}-1}
 *              {[(P_{rated}+P_{in,rated})/P_{in,rated}]^{(n-1)/n}-1}
 * }
 * @formula{compressor-assessment-pressure-factor-power;
 *   F_P=-0.0000577P_{atm}^3+0.000251P_{atm}^2+0.0466P_{atm}+0.4442
 * }
 * @formula{compressor-assessment-pressure-factor-flow;
 *   F_Q=0.000258P_{atm}^3-0.0116P_{atm}^2+0.176P_{atm}+0.09992
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{P_{adj}; Corrected package input power; \kilo\watt}
 * @symrow{Q_{adj}; Corrected airflow; \acfm}
 * @symrow{F_P,F_Q; Empirical altitude factors, or 1 when disabled; \unitless}
 * @symrow{P_{atm}; Local atmospheric pressure; \psia}
 * @symrow{P_{in}; Operating inlet pressure; \psia}
 * @symrow{BHP_{FL}; Full-load mechanical shaft power; \horsepower}
 * @symrow{0.746; Brake-horsepower-to-kilowatt conversion; \kilo\watt\per\horsepower}
 * @symrow{\eta; Motor efficiency entered from 0 to 1; \unitless}
 * @symrow{R_P; Polytropic pressure-ratio correction; \unitless}
 * @symrow{Q_{rated}; Manufacturer rated capacity; \acfm}
 * @symrow{P_d; Operating discharge gauge pressure; \psig}
 * @symrow{P_{rated}; Rated discharge gauge pressure; \psig}
 * @symrow{P_b; Compressor-type pressure-ratio base; \psia}
 * @symrow{P_{in,rated}; Rated inlet absolute pressure; \psia}
 * @symrow{n; Polytropic exponent; \unitless}
 * @symrow{0.00075; Empirical capacity-pressure coefficient; \per\psi}
 * @endsymtable
 */
